   "content": "<h2 class=\"lesson-h2\">Activity 1: Trace Hours and Minutes</h2><details class=\"lesson-tps\"><summary><span class=\"lesson-tps-steps\">Think <b>&rarr;</b> Pair <b>&rarr;</b> Share</span><span class=\"lesson-tps-q\">Why does line 5 use DIV and not / ?</span><span class=\"lesson-tps-reveal\">Show the answer</span></summary><div class=\"lesson-tps-a\"><p>Hours is an INTEGER. / can give a number with a decimal point. DIV always gives a whole number.</p></div></details><div id=\"y11ar-embed-1\" class=\"lesson-embed\"></div>"
  },
  {
   "id": "exam-quotient",
   "label": "Exam Question: Which Operator?",
   "type": "multiple-choice",
   "containerId": "y11ar-exam-quotient",
   "content": "<h2 class=\"lesson-h2\">Exam Question: Which Operator?</h2><details class=\"lesson-tps\"><summary><span class=\"lesson-tps-steps\">Think <b>&rarr;</b> Pair <b>&rarr;</b> Share</span><span class=\"lesson-tps-q\">What does \"discarded\" mean here?</span><span class=\"lesson-tps-reveal\">Show the answer</span></summary><div class=\"lesson-tps-a\"><p>Thrown away. The fractional part, after the decimal point, is thrown away.</p></div></details><p class=\"lesson-lead\">Cambridge IGCSE 0478/21, November 2025, Question 3.</p><div id=\"y11ar-exam-quotient\"></div>",
   "items": [
    {
     "prompt": "Tick one box to identify the operator that returns the quotient of a calculation with the fractional part discarded.",
     "options": [
      "/",
      "DIV",
      "^",
      "MOD"
     ],
     "correct": 1,
     "explain": "DIV returns the quotient, the whole number part. / keeps the fractional part. MOD returns the remainder."
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "exam-not-arith",
   "label": "Exam Question: Not Arithmetic",
   "type": "short-answer-validation",
   "validatorId": "y11ar-exam-not-arith",
   "validatorKey": "y11ar_exam_not_arith",
   "content": "<h2 class=\"lesson-h2\">Exam Question: Not Arithmetic</h2><details class=\"lesson-tps\"><summary><span class=\"lesson-tps-steps\">Think <b>&rarr;</b> Pair <b>&rarr;</b> Share</span><span class=\"lesson-tps-q\">How can you answer this without knowing what AND means?</span><span class=\"lesson-tps-reveal\">Show the answer</span></summary><div class=\"lesson-tps-a\"><p>You know the arithmetic operators. Any operator that is not one of them is the answer.</p></div></details><p class=\"lesson-lead\">Cambridge IGCSE 0478/21, June 2026, Question 2. Identify four operators that are <strong>not</strong> arithmetic operators from the list.</p><div class=\"lesson-do-now-columns\"><div><div class=\"lesson-code\"><div class=\"lesson-code-line\">/    &gt;    &lt;=    +    *</div><div class=\"lesson-code-line\">^    AND  DIV   MOD  NOT</div></div></div><div class=\"lesson-exam-card\"><div class=\"lesson-do-now-responses\"><div class=\"lesson-do-now-response\"><label for=\"y11ar-exam-not-arith-a\">(a) Write the four operators, with a space between each one. [4]</label><input id=\"y11ar-exam-not-arith-a\" class=\"pseudocode-output-input lesson-exam-answer\" data-answer-kind=\"short\" data-answer-id=\"exam-not-arith-a\" aria-label=\"Exam Question: Not Arithmetic part a\" autocomplete=\"off\"></div></div><div class=\"lesson-do-now-actions\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"donow-btn\" id=\"y11ar-exam-not-arith-check\">Check answers</button><strong>Total: 4 marks</strong></div><div id=\"y11ar-exam-not-arith-feedback\" class=\"pseudocode-feedback\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\"></div></div></div>"
  },
  {
   "id": "activity-2",
   "label": "Activity 2: Write the Lines",
   "type": "embedded-app",
   "appId": "drill-y11-8-1-arith-code",
   "embedContainerId": "y11ar-activity-2",
   "content": "<h2 class=\"lesson-h2\">Activity 2: Write the Lines</h2><details class=\"lesson-tps\"><summary><span class=\"lesson-tps-steps\">Think <b>&rarr;</b> Pair <b>&rarr;</b> Share</span><span class=\"lesson-tps-q\">Why is your program tested with new values each time?</span><span class=\"lesson-tps-reveal\">Show the answer</span></summary><div class=\"lesson-tps-a\"><p>It must work for any value, not just one. Then you know your operator is right.</p></div></details><p class=\"lesson-lead\">Write the lines in the box. Press Run. Your program must give the same answer as the model program, with new values each time.</p><div id=\"y11ar-activity-2\"></div>"
  },
  {
   "id": "exam-purpose",
   "label": "Exam Question: MOD and DIV",
   "type": "self-marked-response",
   "containerId": "y11ar-exam-purpose",
   "content": "<h2 class=\"lesson-h2\">Exam Question: MOD and DIV</h2><details class=\"lesson-tps\"><summary><span class=\"lesson-tps-steps\">Think <b>&rarr;</b> Pair <b>&rarr;</b> Share</span><span class=\"lesson-tps-q\">This question has 4 marks. What do you need to write?</span><span class=\"lesson-tps-reveal\">Show the answer</span></summary><div class=\"lesson-tps-a\"><p>A purpose and an example for MOD, then a purpose and an example for DIV.</p></div></details><p class=\"lesson-lead\">Cambridge IGCSE 0478/21, June 2025, Question 5. Write your answer, then mark it against the mark scheme.</p><div id=\"y11ar-exam-purpose\"></div>",
   "items": [
    {
     "id": "mod-div",
     "prompt": "Two library routines used in programming are MOD and DIV. State the purpose of each of the library routines. Give one example pseudocode statement for each of the library routines. [4]",
     "marks": 4,
     "modelAnswer": "MOD returns the remainder of a division calculation. Example: X <- MOD(10, 3). DIV is integer division: it returns the quotient of a division. Example: Y <- DIV(10, 3)."
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "plenary",
   "label": "Plenary: Arithmetic Operators Drill",
   "type": "embedded-app",
   "appId": "drill-y11-8-1-arith",
   "embedContainerId": "y11ar-plenary",
   "content": "<h2 class=\"lesson-h2\">Plenary: Arithmetic Operators Drill</h2><details class=\"lesson-tps\"><summary><span class=\"lesson-tps-steps\">Think <b>&rarr;</b> Pair <b>&rarr;</b> Share</span><span class=\"lesson-tps-q\">Why practise with new values every time?</span><span class=\"lesson-tps-reveal\">Show the answer</span></summary><div class=\"lesson-tps-a\"><p>If you can do it with any values, you really understand it.</p></div></details><p class=\"lesson-lead\">The five operators, DIV and MOD, tracing programs and writing the lines. Your progress is saved.</p><div id=\"y11ar-plenary\"></div>"
  }
 ],
